ng up the dosing for uh  the over the 50s put  any of the other roll out targets at  risk  happily not so what we're doing at the  moment  is using our supplies of the astrazeneca  vaccine  almost entirely for giving second doses  those are available the logistics in  terms of delivery  was feasible and because we've got such  an  amazingly efficient mechanism for making  appointments and people  showing up so promptly and reliably we  can do this  without really having any impact or  negative impact on any other aspect of  the program  if it really is a race and does feel  like it's a race that's what matt  hancock was saying you'll talk about the  importance of getting vaccinations out  as well as quickly as possible  could the jcvi look again at this  recommendation that the under 40s should  be offered non-astrazeneca jabs if it  means that it could speed up that roll  out  yes absolutely that's that's on the  agenda  and if necessary that's something that  could be done when we  expressed a preference for  non-astrazeneca vaccines for this age  group  it was done in a very provisional way on  the basis of everything going absolutely  right  and if the evidence shows that the  risk-benefit balance for people in their  30s  is to be offered that vaccine then  absolutely  that that recommendation will be changed  at the moment we don't think that's  necessary  but it could could well become a  recommendation in the future  that's interesting to hear and also  looking ahead to the future and  president biden  laid out plans last week to vaccinate 12  to 15 year olds quite  soon what's the latest on the policy on  vaccinating children in the united  kingdom well we've just started having  discussions about this clearly it's  something that needs to be thought about  but i think the consensus at the moment  is that children are very much  not the priority very few children  getting ill  uh very limited amounts of transmission  being shown to be  occurring in schools 